hello everyone  a couple of weeks earlier i had made a  video on uh viva preparation and that  seemed to have helped a few guys so i  thought  on a similar line i'll make another  video  for  the project presentations especially the  bachelors and the master's levels  presentations  because in a few days from now here at  iit kharagpur we are going to have  our  presentation rounds  now uh although  the things that i'm going to discuss is  based on my experiences as a student and  as a faculty over the past few years  here at iit kharagpur specifically the  mechanical engineering department i  think  the things are general enough for it to  be helpful  for this video to be helpful to students  in general from other institutes also so  without further ado let's get into the  discussion  so even before one goes into the  presentation the work starts much before  that so  the first thing which students  the the question which which comes to  the student's mind is regarding the  length of the presentation now i think  that  if you are putting something in your  presentation you should be investing a  reasonable amount of time in explaining  that  that slide  so uh for a 10 or 15 minute presentation  i think you need about at least a minute  not less than a minute to explain a  slide  that means that you should not be  putting more than 10 or 15 slides in  your presentation  okay so the first thing to remember is  that your presentation is not equal okay  i repeat it is absolutely not equal to  your report or your thesis  okay so your presentation is basically  just a showcasing uh or a highlighting  of the main things that you have done in  your work  all right so uh  in a technical presentation usually have  we have various sections like the  introduction the  uh the problem statement the  mathematical modeling the simulation  work uh the results and discussions and  if you're doing some kind of experiment  experimental work you have to include  that uh instead of the mathematical  modeling or if you're doing some kind of  mathematical modeling work plus  experiments you have to explain both of  them anyway so for the introduction what  you have to say is the you know what you  have to give  is the  background as well as the motivation for  your work meaning why you are doing what  you are doing if you are not clear about  this point discuss with your supervisor  and write accordingly in your  presentation  next your problem statement should be  absolutely clear to written i have seen  this not being presented  at all  or not very clearly by the students so  this is a very rookie mistake you should  not do this okay your problem statement  should be absolutely clearly written  maybe with the help of a schematic  that's very good and if possible discuss  with your supervisor and see if you can  actually state your problem statement in  the form of a question for example what  is the influence of this on that  okay or how or how does that influence  this something like on that on that line  next for the  literature survey  what students usually do is they list  reference after reference or maybe they  just  wait till the end and they just list out  the references at one go i don't think  that's a very good idea okay what i  personally do is that when i am putting  my background and my motivation for the  work as well as the problem statement i  try to put the relevant references the  primary references the important ones as  footnotes in those very slides  okay so it is immediate it is absolutely  relevant and it's short  okay  next regarding the mathematical modeling  well uh again the students are a little  bit confused here  because  they have sometimes they they have done  page after page of mathematical  derivations uh and sometimes they tend  to put everything together in the in the  presentation again this should not be  done  okay rather  what you can do is you can put the main  governing equations together with the  boundary conditions and initial  conditions  and  even more importantly  you should be discussing the important  length scales and time scales  in that mathematical modeling part  highlight what are the important  non-dimensional parameters if any  okay  now up to this point when you are  describing your work you should  somewhere within these slides within  this first five or six slides you should  try to ensure that  somewhere you highlight very very  clearly very emphatically what is the  novelty in your work maybe right in the  problem statement itself or somewhere  within the mathematical modeling part or  somewhere else okay you should be  absolutely clear about what is the  novelty in your work  at the bachelor's level  it may be a little bit difficult because  sometimes the works are not such great  in scope that there is necessarily a new  thing but there must be something  different compared to the previously  published works which you are doing  so try to highlight that so this is the  one of the worst mistakes that the  students make of not highlighting it in  the front of here  in front of their examining professor  next up  comes the simulation work so if you have  done some kind of simulation the natural  tendency of the student is to go into  great details of describing that  simulation work meaning  what kind of activities they have done  or what are the steps they have taken in  the  software whether it is a free or open  source software or some kind of  proprietary software they spend a great  deal of time explaining what exactly  they have done in that software  okay please do not do this  okay the examining professor is not  really interested in those things  i mean there does exist exceptions  but mostly this is the case  so instead what you should be doing is  that  that particular software  is using some kind of mathematical  backend  okay so maybe it is using finite element  methods or finite volume methods you  explain that okay explain that means you  explain that your work has these these  non-linear equations or these these  coupled equations and these cannot be  solved by hand or analytically which is  why you have to take recourse to such  and such and software and that software  is solving these equations  uh using uh this particular method like  finite volume method or finite element  method  okay that's it  and maybe if it is doing some kind of  time stepping then you discuss a little  bit regarding that's the time stepping  method which the software uh is doing  and please note that uh  you actually have to investigate a  little bit what the software is doing so  a little bit of preparation on that  regard is also absolutely necessary okay  so you must give the impression in front  of the examining professor that you have  not blindly run the software okay you  have kind of understood what is going on  at the back end  next regarding regarding the results and  discussions uh  going back to the initial point that i  had made that your presentation is not  equal to your thesis or report uh you  should not be giving all your results in  your presentation suppose your in your  your report you have 12 figures uh  please do not put all of them in your  presentation okay maybe put a few okay  three four or mad max five  or something on that order  around that so now  the idea here is that if you are  if you're putting a figure you should be  absolutely clearly explaining that  so  you put a panel in a particular slide  and then  as if you are explaining to a blind  person you say that we have this on the  x axis we have that on the y axis and  then we can see the variation of this y  versus the x as if as it is increasing  like this maybe it is decreasing like  that so this kind of a thing as if you  were explaining to a blind person you  should be saying that okay  if there is some physical justification  to that kind of an of a degree of an  increasing decreasing trend try to  explain that that actually shows that  you have not just repeatedly run the  software to obtain some kind of plots  rather you have actually understood what  the results are what the physical  significance of the results are that is  of crucial importance in front of the  examining professor that is what he or  she wants to see actually  next  uh you have to go to your  conclusions  if possible it would be very nice that  you highlight how your the results that  you have found out that ties in to the  novelty of the work which you had  initially stated  okay so earlier i had mentioned that you  should be explaining what is something  what is the kernel of a new idea that is  present in your work and if somehow your  results can be tied in to that uh that  new thing then it would be very really  nice so that initial maybe that initial  question had the novelty itself that  what is the influence of that maybe  nobody had asked that earlier so try to  explain at in the conclusions instead of  giving  10 bullet points in the in the  conclusion slide just maybe give two or  three bullet points but those let those  two or three bullet points be very very  impactful in the sense that you  absolutely clearly show that you have  answered  that initial question  okay so now it is the time for the  uh that insider  secret uh so it's it it's like this  you see uh  everyone has an ego  okay professors have an ego also  now most of the ego of a professor is  tied into his personal intelligence so  we will always think that  we are very smart okay however true that  may or may not be will always think like  that so when a student is presenting in  front of us and we are examining it and  we somehow we don't get it very rarely  we will admit that we are not getting it  because of some shortcoming in our own  understanding okay very very rarely will  admit it rather our  our our reaction will be that we are not  getting it because the student has not  been explained has not been able to  explain to it properly to us so it is  your duty as as as a student presenting  your work to explain your work in as  clear a manner as possible actually this  goes for any kind of presentation the  most successful kind of presentation is  the one where you have managed to leave  your audience  feeling intelligent about himself or  herself because that leaves a feeling of  great satisfaction in his or her mind so  on that note i will end this video all  the very best on your preparation for  your presentation and all the very best  on your actual presentation thank you

